  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Where the data actually lands",
      "items": [
        "GRDB (SQLite/WAL) holds two things: the cache of what the server sent, and the outbox table of mutations that have not landed yet",
        "the Keychain holds tokens and secrets with the right accessibility class — a token in UserDefaults is a breach on a shared device",
        "UserDefaults and @AppStorage carry non-sensitive preferences only, and nothing secret reaches the logs, the bundle or a screenshot-visible screen"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "The path of a read and a write",
      "items": [
        "a read goes screen → use case → SDK adapter → server, and the response is cached in GRDB on the way back",
        "a write goes screen → use case → GRDB adapter → one transaction that writes the local row and the outbox row together",
        "the outbox drains on reconnect, foreground or background task: the rows go out with their idempotency key, and a successful send deletes the row"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "The server is the source of truth",
      "items": [
        "the cache is a mirror, never an authority: the client hides an action it cannot perform, but the server decides what is allowed",
        "a conflict is resolved by the server and the local pending change comes back rejected visibly",
        "migrations are code-defined and tested against fixtures of every released version; a destructive fallback that loses data is a review finding"
      ]
    }
  ]
}